---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import NuclideRef from "../components/NuclideRef.astro";
import { NUCLIDES, halfLifeText } from "../lib/nuclides";
import { DENSITY_G_CM3 } from "../engine";
import { layerTable, HVL_MATERIALS, mm3, cm3 } from "../lib/guide-tables";
import { u } from "../lib/site";

/** ★★ 가이드 글 — 흔한 감마 방출체의 반가층·십가층(2026-10-25 D5-2, 감독 지시 12 ①).
 *  **숫자를 글자로 적지 않는다** — 표와 산문의 두께는 전부 `lib/guide-tables.ts` 의 `layerTable()` 에서 나오고,
 *  같은 함수를 `guide-tables.test.ts` 가 고정한다. 차폐는 **감쇠만**(좁은 빔, 축적 없음)이며 글이 그것을 밝힌다.
 *  ★ 바깥 출처는 이름만 든다: NIST 광자 감쇠 계수, IAEA Livechart(ENSDF) 선 자료, 축적계수는 ANSI/ANS-6.4.3.
 *    넓은 빔 값은 숫자로 옮기지 않는다 — 우리 자료에서 나온 값이 아니다. */
const T = layerTable();
const R = (k: string) => T.find((r) => r.k === k)!;
const c = (k: string, m: string) => R(k).cells[m];
const MAT_NAME: Record<string, string> = { lead: "Lead", iron: "Iron (steel)", concrete: "Ordinary concrete" };
/* 콘크리트는 cm, 나머지는 mm — 같은 표 안에서 자릿수가 지나치게 갈리지 않게. */
const unit = (m: string) => (m === "concrete" ? "cm" : "mm");
const fmt = (m: string, cm: number) => (m === "concrete" ? cm3(cm) : mm3(cm));
const SINGLE = Math.log(10) / Math.log(2);
const ratio = (k: string, m: string) => c(k, m).tvlCm / c(k, m).hvlCm;
const csPb = c("Cs-137", "lead"), coPb = c("Co-60", "lead"), irPb = c("Ir-192", "lead");
const amPb = c("Am-241", "lead"), tcPb = c("Tc-99m", "lead"), iPb = c("I-131", "lead");
const coC = c("Co-60", "concrete"), csC = c("Cs-137", "concrete");
/* 「목표까지 몇 겹」 예 — Ir-192 를 1/1000 로: 첫 십가층 + 둘째 십가층 두 번이 근사다. */
const ir1000 = irPb.tvlCm + 2 * irPb.tvl2Cm;
const ir1000naive = 3 * irPb.tvlCm;
---
<Base title="Half-value and tenth-value layers (HVL, TVL)"
      description="HVL and TVL in lead, iron and concrete for Cs-137, Co-60, Ir-192, I-131, Tc-99m and Am-241, from the full photon spectrum; why narrow-beam values differ."
      schema="TechArticle">
  <h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[32px]">
    Half-value and tenth-value layers for common gamma emitters
  </h1>

  <section class="prose-doc mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <p>
      The half-value layer (HVL) is the thickness of a material that halves the dose rate from a
      source, and the tenth-value layer (TVL) the thickness that reduces it to a tenth. They are the
      quickest way to size a shield in your head: ten half-value layers take a dose rate down by a
      factor of about a thousand, and each tenth-value layer adds a factor of ten. This note gives the
      two layers in lead, iron and ordinary concrete for six nuclides that account for most practical
      gamma shielding work, explains why the values here differ from the single number found in many
      tables, and sets out what they leave out.
    </p>

    <h2>How these values were computed</h2>
    <p>
      Most published tables give one half-value layer per nuclide, taken from the attenuation
      coefficient at a single representative energy. That is exact for a nuclide with one photon line
      and an approximation for everything else. The values below are solved instead for the nuclide's
      whole photon spectrum. Every line in the evaluated decay data above 20&nbsp;keV is attenuated by
      its own coefficient, weighted by the air kerma it contributes, and the thickness that brings the
      sum to one half or one tenth is found by bisection. The line data are the IAEA Livechart
      evaluation of the nuclear structure and decay data files, and the attenuation coefficients are the
      NIST tables, with densities of {DENSITY_G_CM3.lead}&nbsp;g/cm³ for lead,
      {" "}{DENSITY_G_CM3.iron}&nbsp;g/cm³ for iron and {DENSITY_G_CM3.concrete}&nbsp;g/cm³ for concrete.
      This is the same calculation the <a href={u("/gamma-shielding/")} class="underline">gamma shielding
      calculator</a> and the nuclide sheets use, and a test in the repository fixes every value in the
      tables to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The figures are for <strong>attenuation only</strong>. A photon that interacts in the shield is
      counted as removed, which is the narrow-beam condition. No buildup factor is applied. The section
      on broad beams below explains what that means for a real shield.
    </p>

    <h2>The tables</h2>
    <p>
      Each table gives the first half-value layer, the first tenth-value layer, and the second
      tenth-value layer, which is the extra thickness needed to go from one tenth to one hundredth. When
      the second tenth-value layer is larger than the first, the spectrum is hardening as it passes
      through the material.
    </p>
    {HVL_MATERIALS.map((m) => (
      <>
        <h3>{MAT_NAME[m]}</h3>
        <div class="pin-first mt-3" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Nuclide</th><th>HVL ({unit(m)})</th><th>TVL ({unit(m)})</th><th>2nd TVL ({unit(m)})</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {T.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><NuclideRef k={r.k} /></td>
                  <td>{fmt(m, r.cells[m].hvlCm)}</td>
                  <td>{fmt(m, r.cells[m].tvlCm)}</td>
                  <td>{fmt(m, r.cells[m].tvl2Cm)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    ))}
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px]">
      Narrow beam, attenuation only, air kerma from all lines above 20&nbsp;keV. Half-lives:
      {" "}{T.map((r, i) => `${r.k} ${halfLifeText(NUCLIDES[r.k])}${i === T.length - 1 ? "." : ", "}`)}
    </p>

    <h2>Why the first layer is thinner than the next</h2>
    <p>
      For a single photon energy, the tenth-value layer is exactly {SINGLE.toFixed(2)} half-value layers,
      because attenuation is a pure exponential. The ratio in the tables departs from that wherever the
      spectrum contains photons of very different energies. Low-energy lines are absorbed in the first
      millimetres, so the first layer removes them as well as half of the main line, and the beam that
      leaves it is harder than the one that entered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cs-137 shows a small version of the effect. Besides its 662&nbsp;keV line it emits barium K X-rays near
      32&nbsp;keV, which carry little dose but are stopped almost at once. The first half-value layer in
      lead is therefore {mm3(csPb.hvlCm)}&nbsp;mm, a little thinner than the {mm3(csPb.tvl2Cm / SINGLE)}&nbsp;mm
      that the second tenth-value layer implies for the 662&nbsp;keV line alone. Ir-192 shows it
      clearly: its first half-value layer in lead is {mm3(irPb.hvlCm)}&nbsp;mm, its first
      tenth-value layer {mm3(irPb.tvlCm)}&nbsp;mm — {ratio("Ir-192", "lead").toFixed(1)} half-value layers
      rather than {SINGLE.toFixed(1)} — and its second tenth-value layer {mm3(irPb.tvl2Cm)}&nbsp;mm. The
      lines between 296 and 317&nbsp;keV carry most of the dose in front of the shield; behind a
      centimetre of lead the 468 and 604&nbsp;keV lines carry most of what is left. I-131 behaves the same way, with a second tenth-value
      layer in lead of {mm3(iPb.tvl2Cm)}&nbsp;mm against a first of {mm3(iPb.tvlCm)}&nbsp;mm.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical point is that half-value layers are not additive when the spectrum hardens. To take
      an Ir-192 source down by a factor of a thousand, three first tenth-value layers suggest
      {" "}{mm3(ir1000naive)}&nbsp;mm of lead; the first tenth-value layer followed by two second ones gives
      {" "}{mm3(ir1000)}&nbsp;mm. For a thick shield, use the later layers, or better, let the calculator
      solve the thickness for the whole spectrum.
    </p>

    <h2>Notes on each nuclide</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Co-60.</strong> Two lines of almost equal intensity at 1173 and 1332&nbsp;keV make it the
      hardest spectrum here. The lead half-value layer is {mm3(coPb.hvlCm)}&nbsp;mm and the ratio is close
      to the single-line value, so one half-value layer serves well at any depth. In concrete the
      half-value layer is {cm3(coC.hvlCm)}&nbsp;cm, which is why rooms for high-activity Co-60 sources
      have concrete walls a metre or more thick.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Cs-137.</strong> The usual reference source for survey meter checks and calibration. Its
      concrete half-value layer of {cm3(csC.hvlCm)}&nbsp;cm and lead tenth-value layer of
      {" "}{mm3(csPb.tvlCm)}&nbsp;mm are useful benchmarks: if a shielding estimate for a nuclide of similar
      energy differs greatly from these, check the input.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ir-192.</strong> The common source for industrial radiography and high-dose-rate
      brachytherapy. Its hardening means that a collimator or a shielded container sized from the first
      half-value layer will be too thin.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>I-131.</strong> Its main line is at 364&nbsp;keV, and its layers in iron and concrete are close
      to those of Ir-192. In therapy wards the patient is the source, and the body attenuates and scatters
      the photons before any shield does; the point-source figures here are an upper bound for that
      situation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Tc-99m.</strong> Its 140&nbsp;keV line is easy to shield with lead: the half-value layer is
      {" "}{mm3(tcPb.hvlCm)}&nbsp;mm and the tenth-value layer {mm3(tcPb.tvlCm)}&nbsp;mm, so a few
      millimetres of lead in a syringe shield or a vial pot reduce the dose rate by orders of magnitude.
      Iron and concrete are far less effective per centimetre at this energy, as the tables show.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Am-241.</strong> Its 59.5&nbsp;keV gamma ray and the neptunium L X-rays near 17&nbsp;keV are
      stopped by a fraction of a millimetre of lead — the first half-value layer is
      {" "}{mm3(amPb.hvlCm)}&nbsp;mm. The low-energy photons do not count in these tables below
      20&nbsp;keV, and in practice they are absorbed by any source capsule. Because Am-241 is mainly an alpha
      emitter, contamination control matters more than its photon shielding.
    </p>

    <h2>Narrow beam against broad beam</h2>
    <p>
      In a real shield, photons that Compton-scatter do not all disappear. Some change direction, keep
      most of their energy and still reach the point being protected. The dose rate behind the shield is
      the attenuated dose rate multiplied by a buildup factor B, which is 1 at the surface and grows with
      thickness — slowly in lead, quickly in light materials such as concrete and water, and more at
      lower energy. Broad-beam half-value layers, measured with the scattered photons included, are
      therefore thicker than the narrow-beam values here; for Cs-137 in lead the commonly quoted
      broad-beam figure is about 6.5&nbsp;mm.
    </p>
    <p>
      The buildup factor most often used for point sources is the geometric-progression fit published
      in the standard ANSI/ANS-6.4.3, whose coefficients are copyright and are not reproduced on this
      site. The gamma calculator accepts buildup coefficients in the two-parameter Berger form,
      B&nbsp;=&nbsp;1&nbsp;+&nbsp;a·µx·e<sup>b·µx</sup>, from whatever reference you are working to, and
      shows the difference they make. For rooms in medical facilities, broad-beam transmission data for
      the specific radiation and material are published in shielding reports such as AAPM Task Group 108
      for PET, and a design should use those.
    </p>

    <h2>Using the numbers safely</h2>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li>Treat a thickness worked out from these tables as a lower bound. With buildup the real shield
        transmits more, so add margin or calculate with buildup.</li>
      <li>Do not chain first half-value layers for a thick shield of a hardening spectrum. Use the second
        tenth-value layer, as in the Ir-192 example above.</li>
      <li>Check the density. Concrete varies with its aggregate, and a lighter mix needs a proportionally
        thicker wall. Steel is close to the iron figure; cast iron is a little lighter.</li>
      <li>Look for gaps. Joints, cable penetrations and doors transmit more than the wall, and scatter
        travels around a shield as well as through it.</li>
      <li>Confirm with a measurement. A survey of the finished shield with a calibrated instrument is the
        check that counts.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li><a href={u("/gamma-shielding/")} class="underline">Gamma shielding</a> — dose rate behind any
        thickness, the thickness for a target dose rate, and buildup.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/alara/")} class="underline">Time, distance, shielding</a> — stay time once the
        shield is in place.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/daughter-shielding/")} class="underline">When the thing to shield is the
        daughter</a> — why Cs-137's shield is really Ba-137m's.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/positron-emitters/")} class="underline">Positron emitters and 511 keV</a> — the same
        layers for annihilation radiation.</li>
      <li>{T.map((r, i) => (<><NuclideRef k={r.k} />{i < T.length - 1 ? ", " : ""}</>))} — the full data
        pages, with all seven shield materials.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
